# AI-Output Safety Rules

Rules that apply specifically because code is being **generated by an AI agent** (in addition to the
general `security.mdc`). Grounded in the OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications (2025). Full control
catalogue + standard mappings: `../governance/security-governance-compliance.md`.

## Treat AI output as untrusted (LLM05)
- NEVER execute, `eval`, shell-out, deserialize, or render AI-generated output without validation,
  encoding, and sandboxing — regardless of how confident it looks.
- AI-authored code gets the SAME review as human code. Being AI-generated is not a pass.

## Verify every dependency (LLM03 — supply chain / "slopsquatting")
- Before adding ANY package an assistant suggested: confirm it actually exists in the target
  registry and was registered *before* the project started. Roughly 1-in-5 AI-suggested packages are
  hallucinated, and the same fake names recur — attackers pre-register them.
- Install only from the approved allowlist; block packages newer than the cooldown window (30–90
  days) pending review. Pin exact versions, commit lockfiles, use hash-verified installs
  (`npm ci` / hashed `pip`), generate an SBOM.

## No secrets, no PII in prompts or code (LLM02)
- Never hardcode credentials/tokens/secrets in generated code or config — use env vars + the vault.
- Never put personal data or confidential source into a prompt sent to an external model. Prefer
  self-hosted models for anything sensitive; DLP-scan egress.
- Scan every AI-generated diff for leaked secrets before it lands.

## Separate instructions from data (LLM01 — prompt injection)
- When building LLM features, keep instructions and untrusted data on separate channels; use
  structured/parameterized prompts and delimiters.
- Injection defense is **system-wide**, not a prompt sentence: structured/guided output, minimal
  tool permissions, retrieval-source hygiene, and authorization checks around connected systems. Any
  single in-prompt defense is assumed bypassable.
- Assume the system prompt is extractable (LLM07) — never place secrets in it.

## Constrain agency (LLM06)
- Least-privilege tokens for agents; no destructive or production actions without human approval; no
  autonomous prod changes. Agents work on feature branches only — never push to `main`.

## Counter over-reliance (LLM09)
- Mandatory human review of AI code; the author must be able to explain it. Fact-check non-obvious
  claims and any unfamiliar API — models invent plausible-but-wrong code and APIs.
- Label AI-authored commits/outputs (EU AI Act transparency).

## Bound consumption (LLM10)
- Rate-limit and quota AI tooling; monitor token/cost per user and per repo; no unbounded agent
  loops (HR18/HR19 — watchdog + escalation, never a silent hard cap).

## Structured output as a control (HR6)
- For any AI result a program consumes, use guided/typed JSON computed over the FULL input — never
  slice. This is both a quality control and an injection defense (it closes exfiltration classes by
  construction). Prefer guided decoding (e.g. vLLM `guided_json`) to `max_tokens`.
